\chapter{A Chronology with Boundaries}
\label{app:chronology}

Dates identify particular events, not the moment an entire community
changed direction. A publication can follow an implementation; a release
can take years to become common. This chronology separates those kinds
of milestones. The early history follows Ritchie and Kernighan; the BSD
and free software sequence also uses Salus. Later entries supply context
for the modern chapters \parencite{evolution,kernighan-history,salus}.

\begin{description}
\item[1969] Bell Labs withdraws from Multics. Thompson begins the work on
the PDP-7 that becomes Unix. This precedes the first published manual.
\item[1971] Unix is operating on the PDP-11. Patent-document work provides
an early practical constituency. The First Edition manual appears in
November; edition numbering originally follows manuals, not a modern
release-management scheme.
\item[1973] The kernel is rewritten substantially in C. Pipes become part
of the developing environment early in this period. Bell Labs acquires
the Graphic Systems phototypesetter. Ritchie and Thompson present their
system publicly at the Symposium on Operating Systems Principles.
\item[1974] The revised time-sharing-system paper appears in
\emph{Communications of the ACM}. Its description is a snapshot, not a
complete account of every earlier Unix version.
\item[1975] Sixth Edition becomes a widely studied and distributed Unix.
It is the system examined by Lions.
\item[1977] Lions's commentary bears this copyright year. Ritchie's
retrospective discusses both strengths and deficiencies. These accounts
should not be read as documentation for modern Linux.
\item[1978] The early Berkeley distributions establish a continuing
practice of collecting and distributing additions. The Bell System
Technical Journal publishes an extensive Unix issue.
\item[1979] Seventh Edition is released. Ritchie presents the historical
account later reprinted as ``The Evolution of the Unix Time-sharing
System.'' Unix/32V and Berkeley's VAX work belong to the transition toward
larger address spaces.
\item[1982] Sun Microsystems is founded. Networked technical workstations
become a major setting for Unix development and use.
\item[1983] 4.2BSD integrates influential filesystem and networking work.
Stallman announces GNU. These are separate developments with later
intersections.
\item[1984] Bell System divestiture takes effect. The FFS paper is
published. GNU work begins. X development starts at MIT.
\item[1985] The Free Software Foundation is established. NeXT is founded.
\item[1986] The supplied Mach paper describes its then-current Unix
compatibility, memory, IPC, and multiprocessor work. Bach publishes
\emph{The Design of the UNIX Operating System}.
\item[1987] X11 establishes the long-lived X protocol generation.
MINIX provides a separately implemented teaching system.
\item[1988] The Open Software Foundation is formed amid vendor conflict.
The first IEEE POSIX.1 standard appears. The Internet worm exposes risks
in connected systems and their software.
\item[1991] Linux development becomes public. BSD Networking Release 2
is made available. Neither event means all historical Unix source has
become freely redistributable.
\item[1992] Linux adopts GPLv2 terms. USL litigation involving BSD begins.
\item[1993] FreeBSD and NetBSD begin. Distributions such as Slackware and
Debian help make Linux-based systems repeatable installations.
\item[1994] The BSD settlement and 4.4BSD-Lite provide a clearer base for
independent BSD development. Linux 1.0 is released. The
\emph{UNIX-HATERS Handbook} records contemporary criticism.
\item[1995] OpenBSD branches from NetBSD.
\item[1996--1997] KDE and GNOME begin in successive years, contributing
to more integrated free software desktop environments.
\item[1998] ``Open source'' becomes an organized public framing for
licenses and collaborative development.
\item[2001] Mac OS X establishes a new shipping Apple platform built on
Darwin and extensive higher-level frameworks. POSIX and the Single UNIX
Specification are consolidated in the 2001 standards generation.
\item[2003--2004] Raymond's book is first published in 2003; the supplied
printing bears a 2004 copyright date. DragonFly BSD begins as another
independent BSD development path in 2003.
\item[2005] Git is developed in the context of Linux kernel collaboration.
\item[2008] Commercial Android phones appear. Wayland development begins.
These are different responses to changing device and graphics requirements.
\item[2018] Valve introduces Proton for Steam Play, combining Wine-based
compatibility with a gaming-focused integration effort.
\item[2019] The supplied RISC-V xv6 text is dated 27 October. Its section
numbers and implementation details are the xv6 baseline in this book.
\item[2021] Greenberg, Kallas, and Vasilakis publish ``Unix Shell
Programming: The Next 50 Years,'' arguing that the shell remains worthy
of research and improvement.
\item[2022] Steam Deck shipments make a curated Linux gaming environment
available as a prominent handheld consumer product.
\item[2026] The editorial cutoff for this manuscript is 6 November.
Descriptions of contemporary architecture do not assert a universal
compatibility result for every release, device, or game.
\end{description}

A family tree can complement these dates, but its lines need labels.
A line may mean copied code, a rewritten compatible interface, institutional
succession, or influence. Linux's relationship to Unix is not the same
kind of relationship as a BSD branch's relationship to its parent.
A diagram that uses one unlabeled arrow for both relationships can be
more memorable than accurate.
